---
import Work from '../../layouts/Work.astro';
import BrowserFrame from '../../components/BrowserFrame.astro';
---

<Work
  title="NFC MSK"
  category="Growth · Search · Business"
  summary="Search and commerce infrastructure built for an NFC products business — a standalone WordPress theme with redirect management, indexing controls and a consistent product-page structure."
  description="Search and commerce infrastructure for an NFC products business: a standalone WordPress theme with built-in technical SEO — redirect management, indexing controls and structured product pages."
  year="2026"
  role="Owner — business, search strategy and implementation"
  status="Live, in production"
  stack={['PHP', 'WordPress', 'Vanilla CSS/JS', 'Technical SEO']}
  ogImage="/images/nfc-home.webp"
  links={[{ label: 'Visit nfc-msk.ru', href: 'https://nfc-msk.ru' }, { label: 'View on GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/Takeshhii/nfc-msk-theme' }]}
>
  <BrowserFrame
    src="/images/nfc-home.webp"
    alt="NFC MSK storefront home page showing the product catalogue"
    url="nfc-msk.ru"
    loading="eager"
    ratio="16 / 10"
  />

  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    NFC MSK is a business I run selling NFC products — cards, tags, stickers,
    keychains, bracelets, badges and plates. Because organic search is the acquisition
    channel, the site is not a brochure: it is the commercial infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    The site ran on a purchased theme that was heavier than it needed to be and kept
    getting in the way of exactly the things that matter for search — URL structure,
    indexation of near-duplicate pages, and metadata at the template level. Page-builder
    abstractions make that kind of control harder rather than easier.
  </p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>
    A standalone theme with no parent theme and no page builder, where the search
    behaviour is part of the templates rather than bolted on afterwards.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Redirect management</strong> — a maintained map collapsing legacy
      duplicate URL paths onto their canonical versions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Indexing controls</strong> — paginated blog listings are deliberately
      excluded from the index while the articles themselves stay fully discoverable
      through the sitemap and related-article links. Nothing drops out of the index; it
      simply stops competing with the real article pages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consistent product pages</strong> — every product follows the same
      structure: shared blocks, FAQ, pricing, gallery and a collapsible long-form
      section, so the template does the SEO work and the copy does not have to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Editable without engineering</strong> — product content is plain markup
      inside the templates by design, not arrays or shortcodes, so copy can be changed
      without touching logic.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    I own the business, the search strategy and the implementation. The redirect and
    indexation logic exists specifically because I am the one responsible for the
    site's organic performance and needed control the previous theme did not offer.
  </p>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    Full control over how the catalogue is crawled and indexed, on infrastructure I can
    change directly — and a product-page structure that stays consistent as the
    catalogue grows.
  </p>

  <h2>Lessons</h2>
  <p>
    The operational detail that costs the most time is not in the code: the host runs a
    cache and minification layer that does not invalidate automatically, so a theme
    version bump is not enough — the cache has to be cleared explicitly after every
    deploy or the old assets keep serving silently. Automating that deploy step is the
    next thing worth fixing.
  </p>
</Work>
